      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine the final price of the shirt after successive discounts, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the price of the shirt after the first discount (20% off).\n2. Calculate the price of the shirt after the second discount (25% off the sale price).\n3. Determine the final price of the shirt.\n4. Calculate the single percent discount that would result in the same final price.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the price of the shirt after the first discount.**\n\nThe first discount is 20% off the regular price of $30. The amount of the first discount is:\n\\[ 30 \\times \\frac{20}{100} = 30 \\times 0.20 = 6 \\]\nSo, the price after the first discount is:\n\\[ 30 - 6 = 24 \\]\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the price of the shirt after the second discount.**\n\nThe second discount is 25% off the sale price of $24. The amount of the second discount is:\n\\[ 24 \\times \\frac{25}{100} = 24 \\times 0.25 = 6 \\]\nSo, the price after the second discount is:\n\\[ 24 - 6 = 18 \\]\n\n**Step 3: Determine the final price of the shirt.**\n\nThe final price of the shirt after both discounts is $18.\n\n**Step 4: Calculate the single percent discount that would give the same final price.**\n\nLet the single percent discount be \\( x \\). The regular price is $30, and after a single discount of \\( x \\)% off, the price is:\n\\[ 30 \\times \\left(1 - \\frac{x}{100}\\right) \\]\nWe want this final price to be $18, so we set up the equation:\n\\[ 30 \\times \\left(1 - \\frac{x}{100}\\right) = 18 \\]\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\\[ 1 - \\frac{x}{100} = \\frac{18}{30} = 0.6 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{x}{100} = 1 - 0.6 = 0.4 \\]\n\\[ x = 0.4 \\times 100 = 40 \\]\nSo, the single percent discount that would give the same final price is:\n\\[ \\boxed{40} \\]",
