      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the final price of the shirt after both discounts and then find the single percent discount that would result in the same final price.\n\n1. **Calculate the price after the first discount:**\n   The initial price of the shirt is $30. The first discount is 20%, so the price after the first discount is:\n   \\[\n   30 \\times (1 - 0.20) = 30 \\times 0.80 = 24\n   \\]\n   So, the price after the first discount is $24.\n\n2. **Calculate the price after the second discount:**\n   Mary has a coupon that gives an additional 25% off the sale price of $24. The price after the second discount is:\n   \\[\n   24 \\times (1 - 0.25) = 24 \\times 0.75 = 18\n   \\]\n   So, the price after the second discount is $18.\n\n3. **Determine the single percent discount that would result in the same final price:**\n   Let \\( x \\) be the single percent discount that would result in the same final price of $18. The equation for the final price after a single discount of \\( x \\) percent is:\n   \\[\n   30 \\times (1 - \\frac{x}{100}) = 18\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   1 - \\frac{x}{100} = \\frac{18}{30} = 0.60\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x}{100} = 1 - 0.60 = 0.40\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = 0.40 \\times 100 = 40\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts is \\(\\boxed{40}\\).",
