      "text": "To determine the final price after two successive discounts, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the price after the first discount:**\n   The original price of the shirt is $30. The first discount is 20%, so the price after the first discount is:\n   \\[\n   30 \\times (1 - 0.20) = 30 \\times 0.80 = 24\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the price after the first discount is $24.\n\n2. **Calculate the price after the second discount:**\n   The second discount is 25% off the sale price of $24. The price after the second discount is:\n   \\[\n   24 \\times (1 - 0.25) = 24 \\times 0.75 = 18\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the price after the second discount is $18.\n\n3. **Determine the equivalent single discount:**\n   To find the equivalent single discount, we need to determine what discount would reduce the original price of $30 to $18. The amount of money saved is:\n   \\[\n   30 - 18 = 12\n   \\]\n   The equivalent single discount percentage is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{12}{30} \\times 100\\% = 40\\%\n   \\]\n\nThus, the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts is \\(\\boxed{40}\\).",
